WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.220
Since 2014, a working group has been developing

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:08.960
proposals for the rehabilitation of the Kaiapoi River.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.280
Today we are on the riverbank opposite the Kaiapoi Borough School .

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.820
This is a nice part of the Kaiapoi River here Neville,
what's happening here?

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.670
What are the council planning on doing?

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:25.810
What we intend to do is try and plant what
used to be here.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:31.020
As you can see in the background, just over
here, it used to be quite full of raupō and

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.280
oxygen weed in this whole area, right throughout
the river.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.470
That's depleted, and we're not sure why, there's
many theories.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:44.989
There's those around salt weeds, there's those
around changing conditions since the earthquakes.

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:49.129
We need to know the effect of sediment and
how deep, and all of those things.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:53.989
We need to know the effects of what's happened
with the nitrates in the river and all of

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:57.739
those things that the water zone committee
concentrating on at the moment.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.760
A lot of locals have theories around sprays,
so we the information on whether

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.020
they are making any difference.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.360
All of that sort of science is being collected,
as well as having monitors in here to see

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.000
how much salt is being pushed back, whether
the river flows from the Waimak in here are to low

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.840
or whether indeed they are not effecting
us at all.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.740
You've mentioned sediment, how exactly are you going deal with that,

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.140
how are you going to analyse it?

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.390
So what we want to do is dig this out, all
the sediment out of here and then put the

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.650
channels through the river so the water can
flow in the channel.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:35.280
And this will collect the sediment and the
cost of actually re-dredging the river will

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.160
be a lot different to what it used to be because
you'll hopefully be able to take the sediment

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.940
from one place as opposed to right the way
down and round the river.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.100
And we want to collect information from the
public about what would be good areas and

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.380
what's their view on this stuff as well? Not just
the science around it,

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.460
but what are they thinking as well about where these sediment traps and things should be.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.780
Like every river in New Zealand, there's no
easy fix.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.780
Lots of steps, lots and lots of steps to rehabilitate
and make sure we can get things back to, well,

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.860
the way they were, I'm not sure whether you can get them back to the way they were, but as close

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.640
as we can get to what had, if not better.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.460
So how can people have their say?

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.220
Well, right here they can come in and there
will be an open day at Ruataniwha and people

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.680
can have their input that way.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.800
We have the website, we have councillors,
community board members and we really want

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.120
people to have their input.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.860
There are six proposals we'd like your feedback
on, attend our open day on the 27th of April

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.680
or find out more at waimakariri.govt.nz

